You are working on the ExamFlow project.

We are now starting:

PHASE 10 — INSTITUTIONAL INTEGRATION & PILOT READINESS

The goal of this phase is to prepare ExamFlow for a controlled institutional
pilot with the Registrar's Office and IITS.

This is the final planned development phase before pilot deployment.

Previous phases:

Phase 0 — Project Scaffolding
Phase 1 — Authentication + Application Foundation
Phase 2 — Exam Management
Phase 3 — Student/Roster Management
Phase 4 — Room/Seat Management
Phase 5 — Seating Assignment
Phase 6 — Crowdmark Template Import
Phase 7 — Personalized Exam Generation
Phase 8 — QR Code + Administration Package
Phase 9 — Production Hardening

ExamFlow is now functionally complete.

This phase must focus on:

- institutional integration readiness
- pilot deployment
- real-world workflow validation
- SSO readiness
- Crowdmark integration assessment
- administrator usability
- operational readiness
- security review
- deployment documentation

Do NOT turn this phase into another large feature-development phase.

==================================================
1. READ ALL DOCUMENTATION
==================================================

Read:

- doc/prd.md
- doc/Architecture.md
- doc/DATABASE.md
- doc/SECURITY.md
- doc/DEVELOPMENT.md
- doc/AGENTS.md
- PRODUCTION-CHECKLIST.md
- REGISTRAR-WORKFLOW.md
- OPERATIONS.md
- TROUBLESHOOTING.md

Review the complete implementation from Phase 0 through Phase 9.

Before coding:

1. inspect the current architecture
2. inspect authentication
3. inspect authorization
4. inspect Crowdmark template handling
5. inspect document generation
6. inspect QR functionality
7. inspect administration package
8. inspect deployment configuration
9. inspect monitoring
10. inspect tests

Provide a concise implementation plan before making changes.

==================================================
2. PRODUCT BOUNDARY
==================================================

The product boundary is:

Crowdmark:
- exam content
- exam questions
- grading
- grading workflow

ExamFlow:
- exam administration
- student roster
- room assignment
- seating
- personalized exam generation
- student/seat identification
- QR identification
- signature lists
- seating maps
- administration package
- administrative audit

Do not blur these responsibilities.

==================================================
3. NO PRODUCT REWRITE
==================================================

Do NOT:

- rewrite the application
- redesign the database
- replace PostgreSQL
- replace Next.js
- replace Python services
- introduce microservices unnecessarily
- introduce Kubernetes
- introduce AI
- introduce RAG
- introduce machine learning
- implement OCR
- implement grading
- build a student LMS
- build a student portal
- build a mobile application
- build facial recognition
- build real-time attendance

==================================================
4. INSTITUTIONAL IDENTITY

Review the current authentication architecture.

Determine whether the system is ready for institutional SSO.

Potential provider:

Microsoft Entra ID / institutional identity provider.

Do NOT implement SSO blindly.

First document:

Current authentication
        ↓
Required institutional identity
        ↓
User mapping
        ↓
Role mapping

==================================================
5. SSO ARCHITECTURE

Create:

SSO-INTEGRATION.md

Document:

- identity provider assumptions
- authentication flow
- callback flow
- user identity mapping
- role mapping
- logout
- session handling
- account provisioning
- account deprovisioning

If actual institutional SSO credentials/configuration are unavailable:

do not invent them.

Create a clean integration boundary instead.

==================================================
6. USER IDENTITY

Define how an institutional identity maps to:

ExamFlow User

Do not use email address as the permanent internal primary key unless the
existing architecture already does so intentionally.

Prefer a stable institutional identifier.

==================================================
7. ROLE MAPPING

Document mapping such as:

Institutional Identity
        ↓
ExamFlow Role

Examples:

Registrar administrator
IITS administrator
Instructor
Exam staff

Use actual existing application roles.

Do not create unnecessary roles.

==================================================
8. ACCOUNT PROVISIONING

Determine whether users are:

- automatically provisioned at first login
- manually created
- synchronized from institutional identity

Do not implement a directory synchronization system unless explicitly
required.

Keep provisioning simple.

==================================================
9. DEPROVISIONING

When institutional access is removed:

ExamFlow access should no longer remain active indefinitely.

Document the expected behavior.

Do not implement an elaborate lifecycle system unless required.

==================================================
10. CROWDMark INTEGRATION ASSESSMENT

Review the current Crowdmark workflow.

The system currently imports an exam template exported from Crowdmark.

Determine:

- what information exists in the exported template
- what metadata is available
- whether Crowdmark exposes a supported API
- whether the required workflow can be automated
- whether QR/barcode integration is officially supported
- whether submissions can be linked programmatically

Use official Crowdmark documentation or confirmed institutional information.

Do NOT assume undocumented Crowdmark functionality.

==================================================
11. CROWDMark INTEGRATION BOUNDARY

Create:

CROWDMARK-INTEGRATION.md

Document:

Current:

Crowdmark
    ↓
Export Template
    ↓
ExamFlow
    ↓
Personalized Exam

Potential future:

ExamFlow
    ↓
Crowdmark API
    ↓
Submission / Exam Integration

Clearly mark unsupported functionality as:

NOT IMPLEMENTED

==================================================
12. DO NOT FAKE INTEGRATION

Do NOT create fake:

Crowdmark API calls
Crowdmark submission IDs
Crowdmark authentication
Crowdmark endpoints

Do not use placeholder endpoints and claim that integration works.

If API access is unavailable:

document the integration point only.

==================================================
13. CROWDMARK IMPORT

Make the existing template import workflow easy to understand.

The administrator should know:

1. Export exam from Crowdmark.
2. Upload template to ExamFlow.
3. Verify template.
4. Activate template.

==================================================
14. PILOT WORKFLOW

Create:

PILOT-WORKFLOW.md

Define the complete pilot workflow:

1. Create exam.
2. Import roster.
3. Verify roster.
4. Configure rooms.
5. Configure seats.
6. Generate seating.
7. Review seating.
8. Import Crowdmark template.
9. Activate template.
10. Validate exam.
11. Generate personalized exams.
12. Verify sample documents.
13. Generate QR codes.
14. Generate signature lists.
15. Generate seating maps.
16. Generate administration package.
17. Download package.
18. Verify package.
19. Print.
20. Conduct exam.
21. Archive exam.

==================================================
15. PILOT SCOPE

The system should support a controlled pilot.

Do NOT attempt institution-wide rollout automatically.

Use:

one department
one course
one or a small number of exams

as the initial pilot.

==================================================
16. PILOT DATA

Use real institutional data only when authorized.

Development and automated tests must continue using synthetic data.

Never commit real student information to Git.

==================================================
17. PILOT ENVIRONMENT

Clearly separate:

development
staging/pilot
production

The pilot environment should not accidentally connect to development databases.

==================================================
18. PILOT DATABASE

Use a dedicated database/schema/environment for pilot testing.

Do not mix:

test data
development data
production data

==================================================
19. PILOT STORAGE

Use dedicated protected storage for pilot documents.

Do not store pilot documents in development storage.

==================================================
20. PILOT SECURITY

Verify:

HTTPS
authentication
authorization
protected files
audit logging
secure cookies
rate limiting
backup

before pilot use.

==================================================
21. DATA CLASSIFICATION

Create:

DATA-CLASSIFICATION.md

Classify at least:

Exam template
Student name
Student ID
Room assignment
Seat assignment
Personalized exam
QR token
Signature list
Seating map
Audit log

Use institutional policy where available.

Do not invent official classification labels.

If policy is unknown:

mark the classification as:

"Requires institutional confirmation."

==================================================
22. DATA MINIMIZATION

Review every database entity and generated document.

Only store information required for the exam administration workflow.

Do not add:

birth date
personal address
phone number
unnecessary demographic information

unless explicitly required.

==================================================
23. DATA RETENTION

Do not invent retention periods.

Document:

what data exists
where it is stored
what should eventually be archived/deleted

Mark policy decisions requiring Registrar/IITS approval.

==================================================
24. PILOT SECURITY REVIEW

Perform a focused security review.

Check:

authentication
authorization
IDOR
file access
QR tokens
CSV uploads
PDF uploads
ZIP generation
audit logs
secrets
session security

==================================================
25. FILE SECURITY

Test:

malicious PDF upload
oversized PDF
malformed PDF
malicious CSV
malicious filename
ZIP traversal

Ensure the application rejects unsafe input.

==================================================
26. AUTHORIZATION MATRIX

Create:

AUTHORIZATION-MATRIX.md

Document:

resource
operation
role
authorization rule

Examples:

Exam
Roster
Template
Room
Seat
Assignment
Generated Exam
Signature List
Seating Map
Administration Package

==================================================
27. OBJECT-LEVEL AUTHORIZATION

Verify that access is based on:

user + exam

not merely:

user role.

Example:

Instructor A

can access:

Exam A

but cannot access:

Exam B

even if both are instructor-owned objects.

==================================================
28. ADMINISTRATOR UX REVIEW

Perform a complete UI review.

Focus on:

clarity
simplicity
terminology
navigation
error messages
confirmation dialogs
loading states
empty states

Do not redesign the application unnecessarily.

==================================================
29. REGISTRAR UX PRINCIPLE

An administrator should always know:

Where am I?

What exam am I working on?

What is missing?

What should I do next?

Is the exam ready?

Do not make users understand the database.

==================================================
30. EXAM READINESS

Create a single clear status:

READY FOR ADMINISTRATION

Only show it when:

✓ roster valid
✓ rooms valid
✓ seats valid
✓ assignments complete
✓ template active
✓ personalized exams valid
✓ QR codes valid
✓ signature lists valid
✓ seating maps valid

==================================================
31. READINESS VALIDATOR

Implement a centralized readiness validation service if one does not already
exist.

Conceptually:

ExamReadinessValidator

It should return structured results:

{
  roster: PASS,
  rooms: PASS,
  seating: PASS,
  template: PASS,
  documents: PASS,
  qr: PASS,
  signature_lists: PASS,
  seating_maps: PASS
}

Do not duplicate readiness logic across frontend components.

==================================================
32. PILOT ERROR HANDLING

Errors must tell administrators what to do.

Example:

Bad:

GenerationError

Good:

"4 students do not have assigned seats. Complete seating before generating
the administration package."

==================================================
33. SAMPLE DOCUMENT REVIEW

Before final package generation, allow an administrator to review sample
documents.

At minimum:

first generated document
random generated document

If random selection is implemented, make it deterministic when testing.

==================================================
34. DOCUMENT VERIFICATION

Create a verification checklist:

Student identity
Student ID
Room
Seat
QR
Exam content
Page count

==================================================
35. PACKAGE VERIFICATION

Before download:

verify:

expected student count
generated document count
signature list count
seating map count

If counts do not match:

do not mark package ready.

==================================================
36. PACKAGE MANIFEST

Ensure manifest contains enough information to verify the package.

Example:

Exam
Template Version
Roster Version
Assignment Version
Generation Timestamp
Student Count
Room Count
Document Count

==================================================
37. PILOT PRINT TEST

Perform a physical or simulated print test.

Verify:

- QR readability
- student information readability
- room/seat visibility
- no clipping
- no overlap
- correct page size
- correct page count

If physical printing is impossible:

use PDF rendering and document the limitation.

==================================================
38. SIGNATURE LIST TEST

Print or simulate printing.

Verify:

- seat order
- room
- student identity
- sufficient signature space
- page headers
- page numbers

==================================================
39. SEATING MAP TEST

Verify:

- physical orientation
- seat labels
- student assignment
- empty seats
- unavailable seats

==================================================
40. PILOT OPERATION

